      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate the relationship of strictures of the common bile duct (CBD) with pain in patients with chronic pancreatitis (CP) and jaundice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 61 patients with CBD strictures caused by CP of alcoholic etiology were treated by endoscopic stent insertion for 1 year with scheduled stent exchanges. Pain was assessed using a visual analogue scale, and analgesic medication was recorded prior to and after endoscopic treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Endoscopic drainage was successful in all cases, with complete resolution of jaundice during the 1-year follow-up period. Prior to the endoscopic stent insertion, pain scores were 6.8 +/- 6.3. Following stent therapy pain scores were 7.7 +/- 7.7. Pain scores, the number of patients requiring analgesics, the required amount of analgesics, and the type of analgesic medication did not change following treatment of CBD stricture."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Successful endoscopic drainage of biliary obstruction has no influence on pain pattern in patients with CP. CBD obstruction does not cause pain in patients with CP."
        }
      ]},
